# Line-ending policy — load-bearing for the manifest system.
#
# asof_invariant.py binds every fitted artifact to its manifest with a SHA-256
# over the FILE'S BYTES (screening_protocol_amendment_v5 C3). That check is only
# meaningful if the bytes on disk are the same bytes everywhere. Without this
# file, they are not: with no .gitattributes, EOL handling falls back to each
# machine's core.autocrlf, which Git for Windows sets to `true` by default. A
# checkout on such a machine rewrites LF to CRLF in the working tree, every
# manifest hash computed on a LF machine stops matching, and
# `python asof_invariant.py --scan` reports HASH DRIFT on artifacts nobody
# touched.
#
# That failure is worse than noisy. HASH DRIFT is supposed to mean "an artifact
# was rebuilt without rewriting its manifest" — the rapm_v0 class of defect the
# invariant exists to catch. A check that cries wolf on a fresh clone is a check
# that gets switched off, and then the real drift goes unnoticed too.
#
# So: no EOL conversion on any artifact that carries a manifest or feeds one.
# The repository's blobs are already LF (verified 2026-07-31), so `-text` keeps
# every checkout byte-identical to them.

* -text

# Genuinely binary, marked so git never diffs or converts them.
*.parquet binary
*.pdf      binary
*.png      binary
*.jpg      binary
*.xlsx     binary
*.zip      binary
*.bundle   binary

# PowerShell is the exception that wants CRLF: some Windows PowerShell 5.1 hosts
# mis-parse LF-only scripts. These carry no manifest and hash nothing, so
# converting them costs nothing.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
